WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Question 0032: wolf, goat and cabbage,
a student-level question.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.067
Two parts: first the plan by hand,
then code that finds it on its own.

3
00:00:09.867 --> 00:00:14.100
A farmer must take a wolf, a goat and a cabbage
to the far bank of the river.

4
00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.700
The boat holds the farmer and one more,
and only the farmer can row.

5
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:23.200
Without the farmer, the wolf eats the goat,
and the goat eats the cabbage.

6
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.500
How many crossings, at the least,
to take all three across safely?

7
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.333
Part A: find an order of crossings,
and show none is shorter.

8
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:38.233
The idea: the only pair that is safe alone
is the wolf and the cabbage.

9
00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:42.600
If the farmer takes the wolf first,
the goat is left with the cabbage.

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.700
And if he takes the cabbage,
the goat is left with the wolf.

11
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.600
So on the first crossing
the farmer must take the goat.

12
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.133
Crossing 1: the farmer takes the goat.
The wolf and the cabbage stay together, which is safe.

13
00:00:56.133 --> 00:01:00.433
Crossing 2: the farmer comes back alone.
The goat is alone on the far side.

14
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:05.033
Crossing 3: the farmer takes the wolf.
Now the wolf and the goat are on the far side.

15
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:08.567
If he goes back alone now,
the wolf will eat the goat.

16
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:13.167
Crossing 4: the step people miss.
The farmer brings the goat back to the near bank.

17
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:17.700
It looks like a wasted crossing,
but without it there is no solution.

18
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.800
Crossing 5: the farmer takes the cabbage.
On the far side, the wolf and the cabbage: safe.

19
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.100
Crossing 6: the farmer comes back alone.
The goat is alone on the near bank.

20
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.000
Crossing 7: the farmer takes the goat.
Everyone is across, after seven crossings.

21
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.900
The roles can also be swapped:
the cabbage on crossing 3, the wolf on crossing 5.

22
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.300
This is the box on the page:
you pick who boards the boat with the farmer.

23
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.200
A try with the wolf: the box cancels it,
since the goat and the cabbage are left alone.

24
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.600
With the goat, back alone, and with the wolf:
the first three crossings.

25
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:57.067
And now back alone? Cancelled too:
the wolf and the goat stay together.

26
00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:03.600
So the goat goes back, then the cabbage,
back alone, and the goat: seven crossings.

27
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.567
Why not fewer than seven? Each forward crossing
carries one item at most.

28
00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:14.733
So at least three forward, and two returns
between them: at least five crossings.

29
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:20.233
With exactly five, each forward carries an item
and each return is alone. The first must be the goat.

30
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:25.267
But on the second return, the second item
stays on the far side with the goat, unguarded.

31
00:02:25.267 --> 00:02:30.500
So one return carries an item, which needs
one more forward crossing: at least seven.

32
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:35.167
And the plan we saw does it
in exactly seven: the least possible.

33
00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:41.433
Four things, each on one of two banks:
16 states, and only ten of them are safe.

34
00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:47.433
Trap: after crossing 3 there seems to be no move.
Going back alone leaves the wolf with the goat.

35
00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:52.533
Going back with the wolf undoes crossing 3:
we are back where we were after crossing 2.

36
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:59.433
Only the goat is left: bring something back
to the near bank, and don't get stuck.

37
00:03:00.233 --> 00:03:04.833
Part B: code that finds the plan
by itself, without knowing it in advance.

38
00:03:05.533 --> 00:03:10.266
The state: one bit for each of the four.
0 on the near bank, 1 on the far bank.

39
00:03:10.266 --> 00:03:14.799
For example, after the first crossing the farmer
and the goat are far: 0101 = 5.

40
00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:19.033
The start is 0000, everyone near,
and the goal 1111, everyone far.

41
00:03:19.833 --> 00:03:24.566
All 16 states: on top who is on the near bank,
below who is on the far bank.

42
00:03:24.566 --> 00:03:29.366
A state is safe if the goat is with the farmer,
or away from both the wolf and the cabbage.

43
00:03:29.366 --> 00:03:34.399
Six states are ruled out, and ten remain.
In all six, the goat is without the farmer.

44
00:03:34.999 --> 00:03:40.399
An edge: the farmer crosses, with at most
one item from his side. Every crossing costs the same.

45
00:03:40.399 --> 00:03:45.133
So we search breadth-first, BFS:
layer after layer, by distance from the start.

46
00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:47.933
Layer 0: the start, 0000.

47
00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:51.099
Layer 1: only one crossing is safe,
with the goat.

48
00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:55.899
Layer 2: the farmer goes back alone. Back with the goat
leads to 0000, already found.

49
00:03:55.899 --> 00:03:59.799
Layer 3: two new states,
with the wolf or with the cabbage.

50
00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:04.099
Layers 4 and 5: the goat goes back,
and the second item comes over.

51
00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:08.999
Layer 6: back alone. Layer 7: the goal,
1111, seven crossings from the start.

52
00:04:08.999 --> 00:04:13.899
Two paths of length seven: the wolf before
the cabbage, or the cabbage before the wolf.

53
00:04:14.699 --> 00:04:19.299
Now let's write it in Python.
First the constants and the safety check.

54
00:04:21.266 --> 00:04:24.666
The BFS queue: deque
from the standard library.

55
00:04:30.566 --> 00:04:34.366
Each one's bit, and EVERYONE = 15,
everyone far.

56
00:04:38.933 --> 00:04:43.433
CARGO: what the farmer can take:
nothing, the wolf, the goat or the cabbage.

57
00:04:52.299 --> 00:04:56.799
river_safe: the four bits, and the rule we saw:
the goat with the farmer, or away from both.

58
00:05:03.099 --> 00:05:07.399
river_plan: the parent of 0 is None,
and the queue starts with 0.

59
00:05:12.166 --> 00:05:15.699
Take a state from the queue.
If it is 15, we have reached the goal.

60
00:05:21.999 --> 00:05:26.499
For each item: if it is on the other side
from the farmer, it cannot board.

61
00:05:28.266 --> 00:05:32.766
The crossing flips the farmer's bit
and the bit of what he takes.

62
00:05:38.766 --> 00:05:43.266
A safe state not seen yet: record where we came
from and what was taken, and enqueue it.

63
00:05:52.066 --> 00:05:56.466
The reconstruction: back from 15 by the parents,
then reverse the list.

64
00:05:57.166 --> 00:06:01.566
Let's run river_plan, beside the BFS layers
in the box on the page.

65
00:06:01.566 --> 00:06:06.266
At first the queue holds only 0,
and its parent is None.

66
00:06:06.266 --> 00:06:11.599
Take 0. Only the crossing with the goat
is safe: 5 enters the queue.

67
00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:17.399
Take 5. Back alone gives 4.
Back with the goat is 0, already found.

68
00:06:17.399 --> 00:06:22.833
Take 4: with the wolf 7,
with the cabbage 13. Both enter the queue.

69
00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:28.766
Take 7 and 13.
From each, the goat comes back: 2 and 8.

70
00:06:28.766 --> 00:06:34.566
From 2 and from 8 we reach 11. It enters once,
from 2, which was taken first.

71
00:06:34.566 --> 00:06:39.799
Take 11: the farmer goes back alone,
and 10 enters the queue.

72
00:06:39.799 --> 00:06:45.599
Take 10: with the goat we reach 15,
the goal, and it enters the queue.

73
00:06:45.599 --> 00:06:49.999
Take 15, and the break
stops the loop.

74
00:06:49.999 --> 00:06:55.099
The reconstruction walks back from 15 to 0,
collecting what was taken on each crossing.

75
00:06:55.899 --> 00:07:00.933
Reversed, it is the plan of part A:
goat, alone, wolf, goat, cabbage, alone, goat.

76
00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:06.233
Why is it right? BFS reaches the states by
distance from the start, and 15 enters at layer 7.

77
00:07:06.233 --> 00:07:11.233
So no path is shorter than seven, and the
reconstruction returns one of the two shortest.

78
00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:17.533
The cost: 16 states, at most four edges from each.
Time and memory are constant in practice.

79
00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:23.366
Trap: describing the state by the three
items alone. That gives eight states.

80
00:07:23.366 --> 00:07:28.866
But without the farmer's bit you don't know
which side he can take from. He is part of the state.

81
00:07:29.658 --> 00:07:34.458
To sum up: seven crossings, and the surprising
step is bringing the goat back.

82
00:07:34.458 --> 00:07:39.358
Try crossing yourself in the box
on the page of question 0032.
